  "meta": {
    "title": "The Laughter Epidemic That Closed Schools (True Story)",
    "description": "In 1962, three schoolgirls in Tanganyika started laughing and it would not stop. Doctors found nothing wrong. What really happened?",
    "hashtags": [
      "#darkhistory",
      "#truestory",
      "#history",
      "#tanzania",
      "#mystery",
      "#shorts"
    ],
    "sources": [
      {
        "claim": "On 30 January 1962, at a mission-run girls' boarding school in Kashasha (Tanganyika), three girls began laughing; it spread to 95 of 159 pupils (aged 12-18); the school closed on 18 March",
        "source": "Wikipedia, 'Tanganyika laughter epidemic' (read in full); Rankin & Philip 1963 (as summarised by secondary sources)",
        "verified": true,
        "note": "Narration says only 'in March'. Reopened 21 May, second phase hit 57 more, closed again end of June (not in narration)."
      },
      {
        "claim": "Tanganyika had become independent not long before (9 December 1961)",
        "source": "Tanganyika Independence Act 1961 / Wikipedia 'Tanganyika (1961-1964)' (search result); Wikipedia 'Tanganyika laughter epidemic' (Hempelmann: 'had just gained independence')",
        "verified": true,
        "note": "About seven weeks before 30 January 1962."
      },
